{"name":"Distant Wind Chime","hex":"#eaeff2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"distant","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Far off (physically, logically or mentally).","Emotionally unresponsive or unwilling to express genuine feelings."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["This term needs a definition. Please help out and add a definition, then remove the text {{rfdef}}."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/distant"},{"term":"wind","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Real or perceived movement of atmospheric air usually caused by convection or differences in air pressure.","Air artificially put in motion by any force or action."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To blow air through a wind instrument or horn to make a sound.","To cause (someone) to become breathless, as by a blow to the abdomen, or by physical exertion, running, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wind"},{"term":"chime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A musical instrument producing a sound when struck, similar to a bell (e.g. a tubular metal bar) or actually a bell. Often used in the plural to refer to the set: the chimes.","An individual ringing component of such a set."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make the sound of a chime.","To cause to sound in harmony; to play a tune, as upon a set of bells; to move or strike in harmony."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chime"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wind chime","blocks":[{"text":"Wind chimes are a type of percussion instrument constructed from suspended tubes, rods, bells, or other objects that are often made of metal or wood. The tubes or rods are suspended along with some type of weight or surface which the tubes or rods can strike when they or another wind-catching surface are blown by the natural movement of air outside."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Wchime.jpg/330px-Wchime.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_chime","wikidata":"Q1621499"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind","wikidata":"Q8094"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Roman_wind_chime_%28tintinabulum%29_flying_phallus_with_bells.jpg/500px-Roman_wind_chime_%28tintinabulum%29_flying_phallus_with_bells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Roman_wind_chime_%28tintinabulum%29_flying_phallus_with_bells.jpg/1280px-Roman_wind_chime_%28tintinabulum%29_flying_phallus_with_bells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Roman_wind_chime_%28tintinabulum%29_flying_phallus_with_bells.jpg/500px-Roman_wind_chime_%28tintinabulum%29_flying_phallus_with_bells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bronze tintinnabulum, Roman, 1st century CE, British Museum.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoman_wind_chime_(tintinabulum)_flying_phallus_with_bells.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Wind-powered_bell_under_temple_eaves%2C._Banna-ji._Ashikaga%2C_Tochigi.jpg/500px-Wind-pow